WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Speaker 1: Welcome back to The David Pakman Show.&nbsp;
I'm producer Pat Forde, filling in for David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.720
today. He'll be back on Friday. Right wingers&nbsp;
love to boycott brands nowadays. Typically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.640
they do so over the silliest of reasons, like they&nbsp;
boycotted Nike a few years back over the brand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.340
supporting Colin Kaepernick. They boycotted Keurig&nbsp;
because Keurig decided to pull its advertising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:26.040
from a Fox News program. They boycotted Starbucks&nbsp;
because Starbucks decided to hire a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:32.040
refugees in response to Donald Trump's travel&nbsp;
ban. They boycotted the NFL and Twitter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:38.040
Coca-Cola and now Hershey's. Lately, the big one,&nbsp;
though, has been Bud Light because the company had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:44.040
the audacity, the temerity to make a specialty.&nbsp;
Can that celebrated transgender influencer Dylan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.720
Mulvaney. Of course, Disney has also been a&nbsp;
big one because they've been butting heads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.600
with Ron DeSantis over the Don't Say gay bill and&nbsp;
other issues in Florida. It's an exhaustive list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.420
and it seems very difficult for conservatives&nbsp;
to be able to keep track of all the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.860
companies they're supposed to be boycotting.&nbsp;
But every time they hear about a new one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:10.980
they tell themselves, get woke, go broke. And&nbsp;
this is the phrase they use to keep themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:17.280
going and to make themselves feel as though their&nbsp;
boycott is actually working. But the truth is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.740
many of these companies aren't actually feeling&nbsp;
the supposed pain that these boycotts are supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.020
to bring them. And this was highlighted in a&nbsp;
recent piece from Rolling Stone. It turns out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.860
many of these companies are actually doing better&nbsp;
than ever. So, for example, I mentioned Keurig.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:38.760
Since the boycott happens, they've acquired Dr.&nbsp;
Pepper Snapple Group, an $18.7 billion deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:46.260
and the company's annual gross profits have&nbsp;
swelled, reaching $7.3 billion in 2022. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:52.140
example is United Airlines. United Airlines was&nbsp;
targeted by right wingers because of all things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:59.100
the airline decided to commit itself to having&nbsp;
half of its new pilot trainees be either women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.920
and or people of color. And since making&nbsp;
that decision, since the boycott, the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:11.640
recently reported a fourth quarter 2022 profit&nbsp;
of $843 million, which well beat Wall Street's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.200
expectations. Carhart is another one. I didn't&nbsp;
even know about this boycott. You'd think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.400
Carhartt would be a brand that conservatives&nbsp;
would like because it's associated with these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:26.100
rugged blue collar type jobs. But they did&nbsp;
have a COVID vaccine requirement for employees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.560
and that sparked a boycott. Now, the company is&nbsp;
private, so we don't know the profits and too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:37.020
much about the numbers, but they did announce&nbsp;
an investment of $4.65 million to expand its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:42.120
Dearborn, Michigan, campus and its plans.&nbsp;
It's planning on hiring 125 more workers at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:50.400
an average wage of $43.22 per hour. So that is&nbsp;
quite a good wage, if I do say so myself. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:57.240
Disney had a gross profit for 2022, which was $28&nbsp;
billion, a 27% increase from the previous year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:04.620
We're hearing that Nike outperformed expectations&nbsp;
and had a fourth quarter of 2022 reaching over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:11.280
$1,000,000,000. The NFL had a dip in recent years&nbsp;
with the pandemic losing them some viewership. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.740
now the league is unbelievably prosperous and the&nbsp;
individual teams are increasing in value. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:22.320
record viewership among broadcast and streaming&nbsp;
partners. There's soaring in-person attendance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:28.200
So what I'm getting at here is that these right&nbsp;
wing boycotts aren't actually working. And these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.840
companies who feel that they should send out an&nbsp;
inclusive message or have a vaccine mandate or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.240
pay their workers a living wage, things that the&nbsp;
left likes to champion, they should feel as though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.640
they have every day that they have the green light&nbsp;
to do so, because, you know, it's working. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.840
boycotts that they could generate aren't actually&nbsp;
going anywhere. Part of this is the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.580
progressives and moderates outnumber conservatives&nbsp;
in the United States by a wide margin. But I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.200
it's more so that people really just don't&nbsp;
have the patience for these sorts of boycotts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:03.540
and they forget about the boycotts after not&nbsp;
that much time. It's just hard to keep track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.760
of all of it. I mean, listen, all these different&nbsp;
companies that I've mentions, it's what, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:13.680
at least ten, 12 and people are eventually are&nbsp;
just going to get sick of it and lose track and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.400
want to go back to buying the products that&nbsp;
they like. And there's no reason really why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.440
conservatives shouldn't like Nike and Starbucks&nbsp;
and Carhartt and Keurig like everyone else does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.000
So it's just a big commitment that they're&nbsp;
not even willing to follow through with. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.900
that's why we're seeing these boycotts&nbsp;
really have no effect. And if they do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.740
want to boycott companies for whatever reason&nbsp;
they want to come up with, they can do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.080
right? They have that right. They can start these&nbsp;
mass media campaigns. They're free to do so. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.300
is the free markets that they claim to love so&nbsp;
much. But it's time to stop pretending as though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:50.760
these boycotts are having any sort of measurable&nbsp;
effects. And it's certainly time sort of tired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.500
The phrase get woke, go broke, because there&nbsp;
doesn't seem to be any merit to it at all.

